(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-4-valvular-heart-disease-vignettes"]={"title":"Valvular Heart Disease Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 4","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 49-year-old man is discharged after receiving a mechanical aortic valve. Which medication must he be told he will take for life?","choices":["Nitroprusside","Warfarin","Aspirin","Heparin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A mechanical valve of titanium and carbon needs lifelong anticoagulation, and warfarin, the most commonly used vitamin K antagonist, is recommended for long-term management. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 75, 21"},{"q":"A 19-year-old woman is found to have a mid-systolic click at the apex at a sports physical. She feels well. Which is the most appropriate initial test?","choices":["Chest radiograph","Echocardiogram","Cardiac catheterization","Electrocardiogram"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Mitral valve prolapse is diagnosed by echocardiogram, which shows thickened, redundant mitral leaflets greater than 5 mm bowing into the left atrium during systole. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man with known pulmonic regurgitation now has fatigue, ankle edema, distended neck veins and ascites. Which process explains these findings?","choices":["Right-sided heart failure","Left-sided heart failure","Low-output hypotension","Pulmonary edema only"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Retrograde flow from the pulmonary artery overloads the right ventricle, and the symptoms of pulmonic regurgitation are those of right-sided heart failure: fatigue, peripheral edema, jugular venous distension and ascites. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 53-year-old woman with mitral stenosis and an enlarged left atrium now has an irregularly irregular pulse from atrial fibrillation. Which complication does this rhythm place her at risk for?","choices":["Thromboembolism","Aortic dissection","Papillary muscle rupture","Carotid radiation of the murmur"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Left atrial enlargement in mitral stenosis predisposes to atrial fibrillation, and atrial fibrillation is a risk factor for thromboembolism. That is why the rhythm matters beyond the palpitations. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman with mitral stenosis has breathlessness on exertion and orthopnea (breathlessness lying flat). Her valve area is 1.3 cm2, so the stenosis is not yet severe. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Hydralazine","Balloon valvotomy","No therapy","A loop diuretic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Mitral stenosis with symptoms of dyspnea and orthopnea is treated medically with a loop diuretic such as furosemide or bumetanide. Surgery waits for severe stenosis with symptoms or pulmonary hypertension. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 41, 42"},{"q":"A 14-year-old girl with a harsh systolic ejection murmur at the left upper sternal border has an electrocardiogram before cardiology review. Which finding is most expected?","choices":["Right axis deviation","Left atrial abnormality","Left ventricular hypertrophy","Isolated right atrial enlargement"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pulmonic stenosis raises right ventricular pressure, and the electrocardiogram shows right axis deviation and right ventricular hypertrophy. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman has echocardiogram-confirmed pulmonic stenosis that is mild. She has no symptoms and asks about her outlook. Which is the most appropriate counseling?","choices":["She will need lifelong warfarin","Survival is about two years","No treatment; survival is excellent","Balloon valvuloplasty is needed now"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Mild to moderate pulmonic stenosis needs no treatment, and survival is 94% at 20 years. Intervention is reserved for severe disease with symptoms. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with a mechanical aortic valve on warfarin is scheduled for a colectomy that requires stopping it. Which plan should be avoided?","choices":["Resuming a vitamin K antagonist","Stopping warfarin with no bridge","Bridging with heparin","Rechecking his ratio after surgery"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. If surgery requires interrupting warfarin, it must be bridged with heparin until the international normalized ratio is therapeutic; leaving a mechanical valve uncovered is the thing to avoid. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman has mechanical valves in both the aortic and mitral positions. Which target international normalized ratio is recommended?","choices":["3.5","3.0","2.5","2.0"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Mechanical valves in both the aortic and mitral positions target an international normalized ratio of 3.0, range 2.5 to 3.5 -- when two targets differ, go with the higher one. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"A 42-year-old woman has tricuspid stenosis seen on an echocardiogram and mild leg swelling. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["No follow-up needed","Mechanical replacement","Balloon valvotomy now","Cardiology referral"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Tricuspid stenosis is referred when it is seen on an echocardiogram or when there are heart failure related symptoms, both of which she has. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man with worsening tricuspid regurgitation from pulmonary hypertension is being followed. Which symptoms mark that his regurgitation has become severe?","choices":["Hoarseness and dysphagia","Exertional angina and syncope","Hemoptysis and orthopnea","Pedal edema and ascites"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Mild to moderate tricuspid regurgitation is usually asymptomatic; severe disease causes pedal edema and ascites from right-sided congestion. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman with echocardiogram-confirmed mitral valve prolapse has no symptoms and no murmur. She is worried about her heart. Which is the most appropriate counseling?","choices":["Usually benign; no treatment is needed","Lifelong warfarin to prevent clots","Avoid all exercise for life","Plan an early valve replacement"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Mitral valve prolapse is usually benign and asymptomatic, and usually no treatment is necessary. Intervention is considered only if it progresses to mitral regurgitation. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman with severe aortic stenosis needs valve replacement. She has had repeated major bleeding, and anticoagulation is contraindicated. Which valve type is preferred?","choices":["No valve replacement","Bioprosthetic valve","Mechanical valve","Balloon valvuloplasty"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bioprosthetic valves are preferred when there is any contraindication to anticoagulation; they do not need anticoagulation beyond the immediate postoperative period. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 74, 21"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man has an echocardiogram showing a dilated aortic root with mild aortic regurgitation. He feels well. Which is the most appropriate plan?","choices":["Reassurance with no follow-up","Emergency valve replacement","Annuloplasty of the tricuspid valve","Ongoing monitoring by cardiology"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Patients with a dilated aortic root should be monitored by a cardiologist, and a patient with an audible regurgitant murmur should have a cardiology consult to decide whether follow-up is needed. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 47-year-old woman with tricuspid stenosis has right upper quadrant pain. Examination finds a tender, enlarged liver. Which explains her pain?","choices":["A dilated aortic root","Congestion of an enlarged liver","Low cardiac output to the gut","Gallstones from carcinoid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Obstructed inflow from the right atrium to the right ventricle backs blood into the venous system, causing portal congestion; right upper quadrant pain in severe tricuspid stenosis comes from the enlarged liver. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 67, 66"},{"q":"A 39-year-old woman who emigrated from a country where rheumatic fever is common has breathlessness on exertion and orthopnea (breathlessness lying flat). A diastolic rumble is heard at the apex. Which is the most appropriate initial test?","choices":["Cardiac catheterization","Electrocardiogram only","No test until symptoms worsen","Transthoracic echocardiogram"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Any patient with symptoms suggestive of mitral stenosis, such as dyspnea on exertion or orthopnea, should get an echocardiogram. It shows a large left atrium and the stenotic mitral valve. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 43, 39"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman has severe mitral regurgitation. Which electrocardiogram finding is most expected?","choices":["Right axis deviation","Right atrial enlargement","An expected normal tracing","Left atrial enlargement"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Severe mitral regurgitation shows left atrial enlargement and left ventricular hypertrophy on the electrocardiogram, though with low sensitivity and questionable specificity. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 59-year-old woman has a mechanical aortic valve and takes warfarin. Which target international normalized ratio is recommended for her?","choices":["4.0","2.5","3.0","1.5"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. For a mechanical aortic valve the target international normalized ratio is 2.5, with a range of 2.0 to 3.0. A mechanical mitral valve, or valves in both positions, target 3.0. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman has an early diastolic murmur at the second left intercostal space from pulmonic regurgitation. Her history includes a balloon valvuloplasty at age 6 for right ventricular outflow obstruction. Which is the most likely cause of her regurgitation?","choices":["Her prior valvuloplasty","Carcinoid disease","Rheumatic heart disease","Myxomatous degeneration"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Iatrogenic regurgitation, after surgical valvotomy, valvectomy or valvuloplasty to reduce right ventricular outflow tract obstruction, is the most common cause of pulmonic regurgitation. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"A 33-year-old man with severe pulmonic stenosis awaiting valvuloplasty has ankle swelling and an enlarged liver from right heart failure. Which medication relieves these symptoms?","choices":["Warfarin","Nitroprusside","A diuretic","A nitrate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In severe symptomatic pulmonic stenosis, diuretics give symptomatic relief of right heart failure, alongside pulmonic balloon valvuloplasty for the valve itself. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman with acute severe aortic regurgitation from endocarditis is waiting for valve surgery that is delayed by several hours. Which is the most appropriate temporary stabilization in the intensive care unit?","choices":["Oral hydralazine and discharge home","Percutaneous balloon valvuloplasty","Intravenous diuretics and nitroprusside","A fluid bolus to raise her preload"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. If surgery for acute severe aortic regurgitation is delayed, temporary stabilization may be attempted in the intensive care unit with intravenous diuretics and a vasodilator such as nitroprusside. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 21-year-old woman has pulmonic stenosis seen incidentally on an echocardiogram. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Refer to a cardiologist","Balloon valvuloplasty now","No further evaluation","Start lifelong warfarin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Whether congenital or related to heart failure, pulmonic stenosis present on echocardiogram should be referred to a cardiologist. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"A 40-year-old woman with no cardiac symptoms is found to have pulmonic regurgitation. Which is the most common way this lesion comes to attention?","choices":["Syncope during strenuous exercise","Sudden shock and pulmonary edema","An incidental echo or exam finding","Chest pain with a new infarction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pulmonic regurgitation is usually an incidental finding on an echocardiogram or on physical examination; the murmur is soft and hard to hear. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"An 80-year-old man has exertional syncope. Echocardiogram shows an aortic valve area of 0.7 cm2, a mean gradient of 52 mmHg, a jet velocity of 4.6 m/s and symptomatic left ventricular systolic dysfunction. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Transcatheter aortic valve replacement","Nitrates and a beta blocker","Percutaneous balloon valvuloplasty","Repeat echocardiogram in one year"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Surgery is indicated for severe aortic stenosis (below 1, above 40, above 4) with symptomatic left ventricular systolic dysfunction. Medical treatment is not effective and is for mild and moderate disease only. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man has a holosystolic blowing murmur at the apex radiating to the axilla. He is asked to squeeze a hand grip firmly for a minute while the examiner listens. How does the murmur change?","choices":["It becomes diastolic","It becomes louder","It becomes softer","It does not change"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Squatting and handgrip increase the mitral regurgitation murmur: peripheral vascular resistance to ventricular ejection rises and pushes more regurgitation back into the left atrium. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 79-year-old woman has a harsh systolic murmur at the second right intercostal space at the sternal border. Her physician palpates the apex and the carotid at the same time. Which combined finding suggests her stenosis is severe?","choices":["Displaced apex with a collapsing pulse","Normal apex with a brisk carotid pulse","Forceful apex with a delayed, weak carotid","Weak apex with a bounding carotid pulse"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Simultaneous palpation of a forceful left ventricular apex beat and a delayed, weakened carotid pulse is a bedside clue that severe aortic stenosis is present. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 78-year-old man has chest pressure and lightheadedness when climbing stairs. He has a harsh systolic crescendo-decrescendo murmur at the second right intercostal space at the sternal border that radiates to both carotids. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Mitral regurgitation","Aortic regurgitation","Aortic stenosis","Pulmonic stenosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Harsh means stenosis and systolic narrows it to the aortic or pulmonic valve; the second right intercostal space with radiation to the carotids places it at the aortic valve. Exertional angina and near-syncope are cardinal symptoms. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 17, 8"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man has severe tricuspid regurgitation due to annular dilation from pulmonary hypertension. Beyond treating the underlying condition, which procedure is indicated?","choices":["Valvotomy","Mitral clip","Annuloplasty","Valvuloplasty"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Tricuspid regurgitation is treated by addressing the underlying condition, with annuloplasty when the regurgitation is due to annular dilation; repair or replacement is for a primary cause. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman has severe mitral regurgitation with symptoms of left ventricular systolic dysfunction. Her ejection fraction is 52% and the left ventricular end-systolic dimension is 45 mm. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Percutaneous balloon valvotomy","A loop diuretic and yearly echo","Aortic valve replacement","A mitral valve clip or replacement"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Surgery is indicated for severe mitral regurgitation with symptomatic left ventricular systolic dysfunction, an ejection fraction below 60% or an end-systolic dimension above 40 mm. Options include a mitral valve clip or transcatheter mitral valve replacement. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman with rheumatic mitral stenosis is in sinus rhythm. Which electrocardiogram finding is most expected?","choices":["Left atrial abnormality","Right axis deviation alone","Right atrial enlargement only","An expected normal tracing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Mitral stenosis raises left atrial size and pressure, so the electrocardiogram shows left atrial abnormality. Atrial fibrillation is common in severe disease, and right ventricular hypertrophy appears with pulmonary hypertension. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 39"}]};
